When writing unit tests for package validators, we should explicitly assert that all expected checks are present, pass, and are emitted in strict byte-order by ID. This ensures that any new checks added in the future are correctly registered, ordered, and verified.

```javascript
    expect(validatorResult.summary.validation_status).toBe('pass');
    expect(validatorResult.summary.check_fail_count).toBe(0);

    const expectedCheckIds = [
      'backlog_non_blocking_and_high_risk_not_accepted',
      'counts_by_predicate_risk_component_present',
      'no_forbidden_side_effects',
      'no_rag_production_or_launch_claim',
      'prior_child_issue_pr_chain_merged',
      'promoted_assertions_have_provenance',
      'rag_lite_handoff_non_empty',
      'required_artifacts_present',
      'unsafe_layers_excluded_from_answer_facts',
      'v1_safe_semantic_content_non_empty',
      'validators_acceptable',
    ];
    expect(validatorResult.checks.map((c) => c.id)).toEqual(expectedCheckIds);
    validatorResult.checks.forEach((check) => {
      expect(check.status).toBe('pass');
    });
```

